concept strings {
  name     = "Strings"
  summary  = "Immutable UTF-8 text; all operations are methods returning new strings; character-indexed."
  related  = [values, containers, string_methods]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<PROSE
A `string` is an immutable, reference-typed UTF-8 value. Every operation is a method
that returns a *new* string — strings are never mutated in place. Indexing helpers
(`len`, `slice`, `find`) work in **characters**, not bytes (`bytes_len` gives the
byte count).
PROSE
    h2 "Building strings"
    code "rust" { source = <<'SRC'
let log = "hp: {99}"                     // interpolation: {expr} embeds any expression
let who = "player {p.name} at {x + 1}"   // rendered like str()
let cat = "hp: " + str(99)               // + concatenation and str() also work
let msg = fmt("{} of {}", 3, 10)         // fmt: for DYNAMIC templates
let hex = fmt("{:>8} {:.2} {:x}", "hi", 3.14159, 255)  // and format specs
SRC
    }
    p "**Interpolation:** every string literal may hold `{expr}` holes — one full expression each (fields, calls, arithmetic, even struct literals and nested interpolated strings), rendered like `str()`. Holes are real code — write inner strings with plain quotes (`{s.split(\",\")}`), not `\\\"` escapes. Escape literal braces as `{{` and `}}`. For fmt-template compatibility, `{}` and `{:spec}` stay *literal* text — so `fmt` templates are unchanged, but regex quantifiers and inline JSON need escaping: `\"[0-9]{{4}}\"`. Format specs inside holes (`{x:.2}`) are reserved — use `fmt`."
    p "`+` concatenates two strings. `str(any) -> string` converts any value (using its `Display` impl when present). `fmt(template, args…)` substitutes into `{}` placeholders, with optional format specs (`{:>8}`, `{:.2}`, `{:x}` — see the [Prelude fact](fact_prelude_functions)); placeholder count and specs are checked at compile time for literal templates."
    h2 "Methods"
    p "All string methods return new values. Searching, splitting/joining, reshaping and slicing are covered:"
    code "rust" { source = <<'SRC'
let parts = "a,b,c".split(",")          // List[string] ["a","b","c"]
let up    = "hello".to_upper()          // "HELLO"
let yes   = "hello".starts_with("he")   // true
let n     = "x7".slice(1, 2)            // "7" — character-indexed
let maybe = "42".parse_int()            // Option[int] -> Some(42)
SRC
    }
    p "See [string methods](fact_string_methods) for the full list."
  }
}
